[305.947754, "o", "\r\nTwo things in this file are wrong in the same way: a rule is enforced at some doors and not at others. Finding 1 is the sharper one — NullSession exists solely to make every mutation raise a helpful 'no secret key' RuntimeError, and it blocks the seven MutableMapping mutators by name, but werkzeug 3.1 (the pinned floor, pyproject.toml:29) added `UpdateDictMixin.__ior__`, and NullSession does not override it. So `session |= {...}` on a secret-key-less app mutates the null session quietly, sets `modified`, and is then discarded because `is_null_session` skips `save_session` — exactly the silent data loss NullSession was written to prevent. The test suite never touches NullSession mutation at all (no match for NullSession outside tests/test_testing.py:174), so nothing catches this. Finding 2 is the same shape one layer down: `open_session` catches `BadSignature` to degrade a bad cookie into an empty session, but `BadPayload` is a sibling of `BadSignature` under `BadData`, not a subclass, so a cookie whose signature verifies and whose payload does not decode escapes the handler and 500s the request — reachable in practice by changing the `serializer` class attribute (which the docstring at line 296-299 invites) while keeping SECRET_KEY, or by sharing a SECRET_KEY+salt with a service using a different payload format. I want to be explicit that Finding 2 is not attacker-reachable: the payload is only decoded after the signature verifies, so forging it needs the key. Findings 3-5 are code-read only and are of the 'defensible but surprising' kind rather than outright broken. I did not find anything wrong with the key ordering for rotation, the FIPS-lazy sha1, or the Vary/delete-cookie logic.\r\n\r\nfindings: 0 reproduced, 4 code-read, 1 hypothesis\r\n"]
[305.947807, "o", "ID  TIER                             SEVERITY  WHERE                      CLAIM"]
